      "content": "that's basically what i'm thinking. if it's a shell, the tensile strength would have to be insane to keep from collapsing under its own gravity. i was trying to use the formula for thin-walled pressure vessels but i keep getting results that suggest the shell isn't just rock, but some kind of hyper-dense alloy or composite material. if i assume the internal cavity is a total vacuum, the math only works if there's an artificial structural integrity component holding the shell out. can you help me model what that required tensile strength would look like compared to standard basalt or granite?",
